1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,349
A phone beeps in Bolinao, a coastal town in the Philippines.

2
00:00:05,399 --> 00:00:09,350
It is May 2009, and Typhoon Emong is approaching.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:15,180
Emergency teams send text alerts and move residents to evacuation centres.

4
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:17,310
The storm is still offshore.

5
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:23,166
Part of the information behind that warning came from a computer in Germany.

6
00:00:23,216 --> 00:00:27,260
How did it reach a town on the other side of the world?

7
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:29,404
The story begins in Seoul.

8
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:36,060
In 2000, Asian and European partners backed a plan to connect their research communities.

9
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:40,853
A year later, a link between Seoul and Paris went into service.

10
00:00:40,903 --> 00:00:43,770
It carried just two megabits per second.

11
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:49,581
Its name was the Trans-Eurasia Information Network, or TEIN.

12
00:00:49,631 --> 00:00:54,070
On the map, the whole network was a single line.

13
00:00:54,320 --> 00:01:02,030
By 2006, the second phase linked national research networks in ten countries across Asia and the Pacific.

14
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:08,310
A university in Vietnam could now work with a partner in Japan over a shared regional network.

15
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:14,840
European Union funding and contributions from regional partners helped build those connections.

16
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:19,696
By 2010, the network reached eighteen countries.

17
00:01:19,746 --> 00:01:22,800
One of them was the Philippines.

18
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:24,725
Now follow the forecast.

19
00:01:24,775 --> 00:01:30,260
Twice a day, the German weather service ran a global model of the atmosphere.

20
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:39,370
It selected the data covering the Philippines and sent them across Europe’s research network, then across TEIN and the Philippine national network.

21
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:45,340
Forecasters at PAGASA used those data to run a more detailed model of their own region.

22
00:01:45,590 --> 00:01:52,460
When Emong approached, their warnings helped local teams decide where to move residents and position supplies.

23
00:01:52,710 --> 00:01:59,080
In Bolinao, the value of that connection was measured in time to prepare.

24
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:06,610
In 2012, responsibility for the network passed to the TEIN Cooperation Center in South Korea.

25
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:15,030
From 2016, the EU co-funded Asi@Connect programme helped institutions across the region put the network to use.

26
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:18,118
Medical education shows what that means.

27
00:02:18,168 --> 00:02:29,090
Specialists in Japan and colleagues in Vietnam could examine the same endoscopic images and discuss what they saw, while the detail was still on screen.

28
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:38,080
In June 2026, the TEIN Cooperation Center joined research networks worldwide in a new agreement on Earth observation.

29
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:42,800
The work that began with one line between Seoul and Paris continues.

30
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:48,624
And on a coast facing a typhoon, its purpose is easy to understand.

31
00:02:48,674 --> 00:02:52,150
A warning matters when it arrives in time.
